# Everything here is normal text and may be EOL-normalized.
*.sql   text
*.md    text
*.yml   text
*.yaml  text

# The integrity script must be LF. Committed with CRLF from a Windows checkout,
# CI dies with "/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory" — the same
# class of failure this file exists to prevent.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# ...except the source data. In gitattributes the LAST matching rule wins, so
# this block must stay below the extension rules above: placed before them, a
# delivery containing data/client_c/schema.yaml or data/client_b/README.md would
# fall back under `text` and be normalized — exactly what this file prevents.
#
# The 15 source files in data/ are CRLF, and the entire bronze layer is built on
# that fact: 05_validate_bronze.sql asserts zero carriage returns survive
# ingestion, and data/CHECKSUMS.sha256 pins the exact bytes.
#
# Without this, byte-identity would depend on each contributor's git config
# rather than on the repository. Someone on Git for Windows defaults
# (core.autocrlf=true) who merely renamed or re-added a file would commit an
# LF-normalized blob: the checksum gate fails with what looks like a whole-file
# rewrite, and "fixing" it by regenerating the manifest would quietly make the
# CR-stripping logic and the no_carriage_returns checks vacuous.
data/** -text

# The manifest is generated, not delivered — it is LF and must stay LF. Left
# under `-text` above, regenerating it on a machine that emits CRLF (PowerShell
# redirection, a Windows editor) would append \r to all 15 paths and `shasum -c`
# would report every source file as "FAILED open or read" — a failure that reads
# as total corruption when nothing is actually wrong.
data/CHECKSUMS.sha256 text eol=lf
